## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design end-to-end trust from image provenance to runtime policy enforcement, plus canary control and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Content Trust/Notary for image provenance
- SPIFFE/SVIDs via Vault for mTLS
- OPA policies loaded via Docker Config
- Sigver sidecar for per-request validation
- Canary rollout with latency/error thresholds and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo example: verify image signature before starting app
async function verifySignature(image) {
  const sig = await notary.verify(image);
  if (!sig || !sig.valid) throw new Error('invalid image');
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle signature revocation and key rotation in this setup?
- How would you scale the sigver and policy sidecars across nodes?

## Why This Is Asked
To assess practical config rotation in Swarm and application reload behavior.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config rotation with versioned configs
- Rolling updates with update-parallelism and update-delay for zero downtime
- Graceful reload semantics (SIGHUP or /reload endpoint)
- Update order start-first to ensure new containers start before old ones stop

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js reload handler
const fs = require('fs');
let flags = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/etc/flags.json','utf8'));
process.on('SIGHUP', () => {
  flags = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/etc/flags.json','utf8'));
  // apply flags to runtime features
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the zero-downtime rollout in a CI pipeline?
- What failure scenarios would prompt you to revert the config rotation?
- How would you handle config validation before rotation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to add a real, cross-DC capability using Docker Configs, a TLS edge (Traefik), and a simple stateful component (Redis). It also touches observability (metrics) and inter-DC consistency via pub/sub.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant rate limiting with Redis counters
- Docker Configs for per-tenant quotas
- Cross-DC synchronization via Redis Pub/Sub
- TLS termination with Traefik edge proxy
- Metrics exposure for Prometheus

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-rate-limit middleware sketch
const quota = getQuotaFromConfig(req.headers['X-Tenant']);
const key = `rl:${tenant}:${windowStart}`;
let count = redis.incr(key);
if (count === 1) redis.expire(key, windowSeconds);
if (count > quota) {
  res.status(429).set('Retry-After', String(windowSeconds)).send('Too Many Requests');
} else {
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test burst traffic and quota drift across DCs?
- What strategies ensure accurate time windows across replicas?
- How would you monitor per-tenant usage and alert on anomalies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic cross-DC routing control and zero-downtime config updates in docker-dca.

## Key Concepts
- etcd as central config store
- sidecar watching and config generation
- nginx config fragmenting and graceful reload
- cross-DC raft replication
- observability via Prometheus

## Code Example

```yaml
# example fragment regenerated by sidecar
server {
  listen 443 ssl;
  server_name tenant.example.com;
  location / {
    proxy_pass http://api-primary:8080;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle watch failures or conflicts?
- How to scale the sidecar to many tenants?
